  slide "Locust: stats merge and stop semantics" {
    eyebrow: "Locust architecture"
    title: "Stats push every few seconds; stop drains only between tasks"
    lede: "Every worker pushes its own already-aggregated stats to the coordinator on a fixed, short interval, and the coordinator merges them into a running total. Stopping is a real drain, but only if a grace period is configured, and only up to the next task boundary."

    narration: "Each worker process runs a background loop: send a stats snapshot, then pause on a fixed interval of a few seconds, forever. Each snapshot already contains that worker's own aggregated statistics; the coordinator doesn't recompute from raw samples, it merges each worker's total into its own running total on that same short cadence. On stopping, Locust does have a genuine core drain mechanism, not just something a user script has to build: if a grace period is configured, a stopping simulated user's state flips to a stopping flag, and the per-user loop checks that flag and exits only after the CURRENT task returns — not immediately. The coordinator then waits, bounded by that grace period plus a safety margin, for every worker's active-user count to actually reach zero before proceeding. But that protection has an exact edge: if a green thread happens to be paused between tasks — not executing one — when the stop signal arrives, it's terminated immediately regardless of the configured grace period, because there's no task in progress to let finish. And if no grace period is configured at all, every green thread is terminated immediately, full stop, no exceptions."

    term: {
      word: "bounded drain"
      meaning: "Locust's core graceful-stop mechanism: when a grace period is configured, a stopping user finishes its current task before exiting, bounded by that period. It does not protect a user that is currently paused between tasks when the stop signal arrives — that green thread is terminated immediately regardless."
    }

    points: [
      "Each worker reports its own aggregated stats on a fixed, short cadence — a few seconds, not per-request.",
      "The coordinator merges incrementally into a running total rather than recomputing from raw samples.",
      "With a grace period configured: the stop signal is checked at the next task boundary, not mid-request.",
      "Without a grace period, or if paused between tasks when stopped: immediate termination, no drain at all."
    ]

    caption: "Report every few seconds, merge incrementally; drain protects the task, not the pause."

    render: @scene {
      sdk.Header(
        id = "s4-hdr",
        title = "PERIODIC STATS PUSH · BOUNDED DRAIN",
        caption = "Report every few seconds, merge incrementally; drain protects the task, not the pause.",
        x = 18, y = 16, width = 664, height = 44,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.secondary),
        inkRole = theme(ink.secondary)
      )
      sdk.Panel(
        id = "s4-worker",
        title = "Worker stats loop",
        detail = "send → pause → repeat, every few seconds",
        x = 60, y = 100, width = 240, height = 80,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.elevated),
        strokeRole = theme(accent.secondary)
      )
      sdk.Edge(
        id = "s4-e1",
        mode = "connector",
        from = { nodeId: "s4-worker", anchor: "e" },
        to = { nodeId: "s4-merge", anchor: "w" },
        style = { stroke: theme(accent.tertiary), strokeWidth: 2.2 }
      )
      sdk.Panel(
        id = "s4-merge",
        title = "Incremental merge",
        detail = "into the coordinator's running total",
        x = 400, y = 100, width = 240, height = 80,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.elevated),
        strokeRole = theme(accent.tertiary)
      )
      sdk.Panel(
        id = "s4-inTask",
        title = "Executing a task",
        detail = "grace period: finishes, then exits",
        x = 60, y = 230, width = 260, height = 70,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.elevated),
        strokeRole = theme(accent.green)
      )
      sdk.Panel(
        id = "s4-inWait",
        title = "Paused between tasks",
        detail = "terminated immediately, no exception",
        x = 380, y = 230, width = 260, height = 70,
        surfaceRole = theme(surface.elevated),
        strokeRole = theme(accent.warning)
      )
      sdk.Note(
        id = "s4-note",
        text = "No grace period configured at all: every green thread terminated immediately, task or not",
        x = 60, y = 330, width = 580, height = 30
      )
      sdk.Signal(
        id = "s4-motion",
        from = { nodeId: "s4-inTask", anchor: "center" },
        to = { nodeId: "s4-inWait", anchor: "center" },
        opacity = 0.4,
        strokeWidth = 2,
        label = "protected vs not",
        style = { stroke: theme(accent.warning) }
      )

      timeline main {
        at 0 reveal s4-hdr duration 260
        after s4-hdr reveal s4-worker duration 260
        after s4-worker trace s4-e1 duration 280
        after s4-e1 reveal s4-merge duration 260
        after s4-merge reveal s4-inTask duration 260
        after s4-inTask reveal s4-inWait duration 260
        after s4-inWait reveal s4-note duration 260
        after s4-note trace s4-motion duration 800
      }
    }
  }

  slide "AIPerf: process topology" {
    eyebrow: "AIPerf architecture"
    title: "One coordinator process launches and hosts everything else"
    lede: "The command-line invocation becomes a coordinator process, which starts several internal message-routing proxies inside itself, then forks a handful of always-needed processes as real operating-system processes — and worker processes are spawned dynamically, on command, after the run starts."

    narration: "The coordinator process is where a benchmark run actually lands — it hosts its internal message-routing proxies directly inside itself, not as separate processes, and forks every other required service as a genuine, separate operating-system process: a dataset-loading process, a timing and dispatch process, a worker-orchestration process, and a results-processing process are always present, plus optional processes for telemetry, server-side metrics, network-latency probing, and an API server, depending on configuration. Worker processes themselves are not part of that initial fixed set — they're spawned dynamically in response to an internal command, computed after the worker-orchestration process sizes itself based on available CPU, and the coordinator process is the one that actually creates each worker as a new process. A results-processing tier scales alongside the worker count automatically."

    term: {
      word: "coordinator process"
      meaning: "The top-level AIPerf process — what a benchmark invocation actually becomes. It hosts the run's internal message-routing proxies in-process and owns the logic that forks every other service as a real operating-system process, including workers, which it spawns dynamically on command rather than at startup."
    }

    points: [
      "The benchmark invocation becomes the coordinator process; its internal proxies run inside it, not as separate processes.",
      "Always forked as separate processes: dataset loading, timing/dispatch, worker orchestration, results processing.",
      "Worker processes are spawned dynamically on an internal command, not at startup — the coordinator does the actual forking.",
      "A results-processing tier automatically scales its process count alongside the worker count."
    ]

    caption: "One process hosts the proxies and forks the rest — workers spawn on command."

    render: @scene {
      sdk.Header(
        id = "s5-hdr",
        title = "ONE COORDINATOR FORKS THE FLEET",
        caption = "One process hosts the proxies and forks the rest — workers spawn on command.",
