  slide "AIPerf: the credit lifecycle" {
    eyebrow: "AIPerf architecture"
    title: "Created, gated, dispatched, stuck to a conversation, returned"
    lede: "A credit is built and handed to the credit router, which won't dispatch anything until every worker has announced it's ready. Multi-turn conversations stick to whichever worker handled the first turn, so session state and cache locality survive the whole conversation."

    narration: "A credit's life starts by acquiring a concurrency slot and incrementing a sent counter, then building the credit and handing it to the router. Before any of that can happen, the run has already waited for at least one worker to announce readiness, with a timeout — a startup gate that exists specifically to close the race where a credit could be issued before any worker exists to receive it. Each worker announces itself once, on start; the router treats that as the signal to register the worker and, on the very first one, to unblock anything waiting on worker availability. Dispatch itself has a memory: the router keeps a map from conversation identifier to worker, and if a credit belongs to a conversation whose earlier turn already landed on a specific worker, it's routed there again — so that worker's cached session state, and any resulting locality on the inference server's side, both stay warm across the whole conversation. Only a conversation's first turn goes through least-loaded worker selection; the sticky mapping is created only if more turns are coming, and torn down on the conversation's final turn. When a worker finishes, it returns the credit over a completely separate one-way channel, not back over the dispatch connection, so the dispatch direction never has to double as a return path."

    term: {
      word: "sticky routing"
      meaning: "The credit router's policy of routing every subsequent turn of a multi-turn conversation to the same worker that handled its first turn, so per-conversation session state and any resulting inference-server cache locality persist for the conversation's duration. Only first turns use least-loaded worker selection."
    }

    points: [
      "A startup readiness check blocks the very first credit until at least one worker has announced it's ready.",
      "First turn of a conversation: least-loaded worker selection. Later turns: routed back to the same worker.",
      "The sticky mapping exists only while more turns are expected, and is torn down on the conversation's final turn.",
      "Credit dispatch and credit return travel over two separate, one-directional channels."
    ]

    caption: "First turn picks the worker; every later turn goes back to the same one."

    render: @scene {
      sdk.Header(
        id = "s7-hdr",
        title = "CREATE → GATE → STICK → RETURN",
        caption = "First turn picks the worker; every later turn goes back to the same one.",
